Direct Laser Writing Is Expanding the Technology Landscape

Direct laser writing is an important technology within maskless lithography. It enables users to produce or modify patterns through digitally controlled exposure, supporting customized fabrication and prototyping applications.

The market also includes electron beam lithography and other maskless lithography technologies. Different approaches provide varying capabilities depending on patterning requirements, application needs and system configuration.

MEMS and Microfluidics Create Additional Demand

MEMS and microfluidics represent important application areas for maskless lithography systems. MEMS development requires precise fabrication of structures used in sensors and other microsystems.

Microfluidics applications involve small-scale structures used in laboratory devices, research systems and lab-on-a-chip technologies. Maskless lithography can support development processes requiring customized patterns and design modifications.

These applications broaden the market beyond conventional semiconductor manufacturing.

Research and Prototyping Support Technology Adoption

Research laboratories and fabrication environments require technologies capable of supporting flexible design development. Maskless lithography can reduce reliance on physical mask preparation when designs change frequently.

This capability makes the technology relevant to specialized fabrication, materials research, optical-device development and other applications involving customized microstructures.

